SH_ITER`:\n- `el` = client2, `tmp` = client1.\n- `client__free_item(data, client2)`:\n  - `dynsec_clients__find(data, \"a\")` - finds client2 (head of bucket)\n  - `HASH_DEL(data->clients, client2)` - removes client2\n  - frees client2\n- Next: `el` = client1, `tmp` = NULL.\n- `client__free_item(data, client1)`:\n  - `dynsec_clients__find(data, \"a\")` - finds client1\n  - `HASH_DEL(data->clients, client1)` - removes client1\n  - frees client1\n\nThis seems fine. But what if `HASH_DEL` of client2 causes the head to change, and `tmp` (client1) is still valid?\n\nActually, `tmp` was set to client1 before the body ran. After `HASH_DEL(client2)`, the head becomes client1. Then `el = tmp = client1`. This is fine.\n\nHmm, let me think about a scenario with THREE clients with the same username.\n\n1. client1 added. Head = client1.\n2. client2 added. Head = client2, client2->next = client1.\n3. client3 added. Head = client3, client3->next = client2, client2->next = client1.\n\n`HASH_ITER`:\n- `el` = client3, `tmp` = client2.\n- `client__free_item(data, client3)`:\n  - `dynsec_clients__find(data, \"a\")` - finds client3 (head of bucket)\n  - `HASH_DEL(data->clients, client3)` - removes client3\n  - frees client3\n- Next: `el` = client2, `tmp` = client1.\n- `client__free_item(data, client2)`:\n  - `dynsec_clients__find(data, \"a\")` - finds client2\n  - `HASH_DEL(data->clients, client2)` - removes client2\n  - frees client2\n- Next: `el` = client1, `tmp` = NULL.\n- `client__free_item(data, client1)`:\n  - `dynsec_clients__find(data, \"a\")` - finds client1\n  - `HASH_DEL(data->clients, client1)` - removes client1\n  - frees client1\n\nThis also seems fine.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is not with duplicate usernames but with the `HASH_ADD` key length vs `HASH_FIND` key length mismatch.\n\nIn `dynsec_clients__config_load`:\n```c\nusername_len = strlen(jtmp->valuestring);\n...\nHASH_ADD(hh, data->clients, username, username_len, client);\n```\n\nThe key is `username` (flexible array member), and the key length is `username_len`.\n\nIn `dynsec_clients__find`:\n```c\nHASH_FIND(hh, data->clients, username, strlen(username), client);\n```\n\nThe key length is `strlen(username)`.\n\nNow, `strncpy(client->username, jtmp->valuestring, username_len)` copies `username_len` bytes. Since calloc zeroes the memory, `client->username[username_len]` is '\\0'. So `strlen(client->username) == username_len`.\n\nBut wait - what if `jtmp->valuestring` contains an embedded null byte? `strlen` would stop at the first null. So `username_len` would be the length up to the first null. And `strncpy` would copy `username_len` bytes, which includes the null terminator. So `client->username` would be the string up to the first null.\n\nHmm, that doesn't seem to cause an issue either.\n\nLet me look at the actual fix more carefully. The fix adds:\n```c\nclient = dynsec_clients__find(username);\nif(client){\n    continue;\n}\n```\n\nThis prevents duplicate clients from being added. So the bug is definitely about duplicate usernames.\n\nLet me think about what happens with duplicate usernames more carefully. The key insight might be in how `HASH_ITER` and `HASH_DEL` interact.\n\nActually, let me look at the uthash `HASH_ITER` macro more carefully. In uthash, `HASH_ITER` is defined as:\n\n```c\n#define HASH_ITER(hh, head, el, tmp) \\\nfor((el)=(head); (el) && ((tmp)=(HASH_NEXT(hh,(el))), 1); (el)=(tmp))\n```\n\nAnd `HASH_NEXT` is:\n```c\n#define HASH_NEXT(hh, addel) ((addel)->hh.next)\n```\n\nSo `tmp` is set to `el->hh.next` at the start of each iteration.\n\nNow, `HASH_DEL` in uthash:\n```c\n#define HASH_DELETE(hh, head, delptr) \\\n    HASH_DEL(hh, head, delptr)\n```\n\nAnd `HASH_DEL`:\n```c\n#define HASH_DEL(hh, head, delptr) \\\n    if ((delptr)->hh.prev) { \\\n        HASH_TO_BKT(hh, (delptr)->hh.hashv, bkt); \\\n        (delptr)->hh.prev->hh.next = (delptr)->hh.next; \\\n    } else { \\\n        DECLTYPE_ASSIGN(head, (delptr)->hh.next); \\\n    } \\\n    if ((delptr)->hh.next) { \\\n        (delptr)->hh.next->hh.prev = (delptr)->hh.prev; \\\n    } \\\n    HASH_TO_BKT(hh, (delptr)->hh.hashv, bkt); \\\n    (delptr)->hh.next = NULL; \\\n    (delptr)->hh.prev = NULL;\n```\n\nSo `HASH_DEL` updates the `next` and `prev` pointers of adjacent elements.\n\nNow, the key question: when `HASH_ITER` is iterating and the body deletes an element, does the iteration continue correctly?\n\nThe `HASH_ITER` macro:\n```c\nfor((el)=(head); (el) && ((tmp)=(HASH_NEXT(hh,(el))), 1); (el)=(tmp))\n```\n\nAt the start of each iteration, `tmp` is set to `el->hh.next`. If the body deletes `el`, `tmp` still points to the next element. But if the body deletes `tmp` (the next element), then `tmp` becomes a dangling pointer.\n\nIn `client__free_item`, `HASH_DEL` deletes `client_found`, which is found by `dynsec_clients__find`. If `client_found == tmp` (the next element in iteration), then `tmp` becomes a dangling pointer.\n\nSo the scenario is:\n1. Two clients with the same username \"a\".\n2. `HASH_ITER` starts at client1 (head), `tmp` = client2.\n3. `client__free_item(data, client1)`:\n   - `dynsec_clients__find(data, \"a\")` - finds client2 (not client1!)\n   - `HASH_DEL(data->clients, client2)` - removes client2\n   - frees client1\n4. Next iteration: `el` = client2 (freed!), `tmp` = HASH_NEXT(client2) - reads freed memory!\n\nBut would `dynsec_clients__find` find client2 instead of client1? It depends on the bucket chain order.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. In uthash, `HASH_ADD` adds new elements to the head of the bucket. So if client1 is added first, then client2:\n- Bucket chain: client2 -> client1\n- Head of hash: client2\n\n`HASH_ITER`:\n- `el` = client2, `tmp` = client1.\n- `client__free_item(data, client2)`:\n  - `dynsec_clients__find(data, \"a\")` - finds client2 (head of bucket)\n  - `HASH_DEL(data->clients, client2)` - removes client2\n  - frees client2\n- Next: `el` = client1, `tmp` = NULL.\n- `client__free_item(data, client1